-- | The NTT manager: one contract per deployment, carrying the deployment's
-- configuration and the three value-moving choices — 'Transfer' (outbound
-- send), 'Release' (inbound unlock) and 'Mint' (inbound mint).
--
-- The manager drives the CIP-56 token-standard interfaces ('Holding',
-- 'TIV1.TransferFactory', 'BurnMintFactory') directly, so any conforming token
-- works without a per-token wrapper contract.
--
-- Four parties sign the manager, and each signature has one job:
--
--   * @namespace@ is the deployment's stable identity. It is in the manager
--     and transceiver address preimages, owns the transceiver 'Emitter', names
--     the 'LockedLedger', and scopes replay protection ('VerifyAndConsumeVAA'
--     runs with @consumer = namespace@). It never changes and never acts after
--     registration; it must be durable (losing the party means a new
--     deployment and a migration).
--   * @admin@ is the operational role: it maintains the peer table, rotates
--     the committed factory, and owns the lock/unlock reserve. It is
--     transferable ('TransferAdmin'); transferring it to gg is the guardian
--     quorum's custody opt-in. At registration, namespace and admin may be the
--     same party.
--   * @guardianGovernance@ (gg), the guardians' k-of-n threshold party, is the
--     mint authority for burn/mint instruments and the quorum end-state of
--     'TransferAdmin': a gg-adminned deployment's reserve is quorum-owned and
--     its admin operations are governance actions.
--   * @operator@ ties the deployment to its core bridge instance.
--
-- gg signs actively only once, at the ceremony that creates the
-- 'Wormhole.Ntt.Governance.NttGovernance' root; every manager inherits gg's
-- signature from that root. Value can only move inside the manager's fixed
-- choice bodies, and both inbound choices verify and consume a VAA first, so
-- no signatory can move bridged value alone, and relaying stays
-- permissionless: any executor may submit, but only the VAA-bound transfer can
-- happen.
--
-- Lock/unlock custody is owned by the current @admin@. A deployment registers
-- fully operational and explicitly custodial — the admin owns the reserve and
-- its users trust it, like a custodial bridge. The guardian quorum can later
-- opt in via 'TransferAdmin' to gg, after which the reserve is quorum-owned
-- and every inbound movement is gated only by VAA verification. Isolation
-- between deployments follows from ownership: reserves of distinct admins
-- cannot mix, and the gg-owned pool is shared only among deployments the
-- quorum accepted. Ownership survives any registry-side reorganization of
-- holdings, which is why the partition is by owner and not by holding
-- topology. Each lock/unlock deployment also keeps a 'LockedLedger' and can
-- never release more than it locked. Burn/mint deployments are isolated by
-- instrument: the instrument id is hash-bound to the deployment namespace
-- ('nttInstrumentIdFor'), so no second deployment can register against an
-- instrument that is already bridged.
--
-- The ledger cap is wire-level accounting, not physical solvency. On a
-- registry that charges the sender fees or ages holdings, every release and
-- consolidation pays those costs out of the reserve, so the pot drifts below
-- the ledger balance over time. The custodian covers the drift by passing
-- extra holdings of its own to 'ConsolidateCustody', and 'TransferAdmin'
-- refuses to hand over a reserve that no longer covers the balance.
--
-- Because every choice body carries all four signatures, custody paths must
-- never rely on ambient authority: 'sumCustodyHoldings' checks that each
-- custody holding the manager spends is owned by the deployment's own current
-- @admin@, so one deployment's choices can never spend another custodian's
-- holdings even when that party's authority is in scope.
--
-- The registry factory is deployment config, not per-call input. The token's
-- factory is a stateless service contract whose cid is stable (its choices are
-- nonconsuming), so the manager commits it at registration and uses that cid
-- for every operation. A caller therefore cannot route a lock, burn, unlock,
-- or mint through a look-alike factory to fake a token movement. The
-- commitment is typed by mode ('NttFactory'), matched against 'NttTokenConfig'
-- at registration and rotation, so no call site coerces a contract id. @admin@
-- refreshes it with 'SetFactory' on the rare occasion the registry is
-- upgraded; on a gg-adminned deployment that rotation is automatically a
-- governance action.
module Wormhole.Ntt.Manager where

import DA.Crypto.Text (keccak256, toHex)
import DA.Map (Map)
import DA.Map qualified as Map
import DA.Optional (fromSomeNote)

import Splice.Api.Token.AllocationV1 (Allocation)
import Splice.Api.Token.HoldingV1 (Holding, InstrumentId (..))
import Splice.Api.Token.MetadataV1 (ExtraArgs (..))
import Splice.Api.Token.BurnMintV1
  (BurnMintFactory, BurnMintFactory_BurnMint (..), BurnMintOutput (..))
import Splice.Api.Token.TransferInstructionV1 qualified as TIV1
import Wormhole.Core.Bytes
import Wormhole.Core.Replay (ReplayNode)
import Wormhole.Core.State
  ( Emitter, CoreState, WormholeMessage
  , GetGuardianGovernance (..), VerifyAndConsumeVAA (..), PublishMessage (..) )
import Wormhole.Core.VAA ()   -- HasField instances for VAA record-dot access
import Wormhole.Ntt.Amount
import Wormhole.Ntt.Cip56
import Wormhole.Ntt.Deposit
import Wormhole.Ntt.Ledger
import Wormhole.Ntt.Payload

-- | How the deployment holds bridged value: custody it (lock on send, unlock
-- on receive) or destroy and recreate it (burn on send, mint on receive). One
-- mode per deployment, fixed at registration.
data NttTokenConfig
  = LockUnlock
  | BurnMint
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | The deployment's committed registry factory, typed by mode, so a
-- lock/unlock deployment cannot even hold a burn/mint factory cid (or vice
-- versa) and no call site needs an unchecked contract-id coercion.
data NttFactory
  = TransferFactoryCid (ContractId TIV1.TransferFactory)
  | BurnMintFactoryCid (ContractId BurnMintFactory)
  deriving (Eq, Show)

-- | Does the committed factory's type match the deployment mode? Checked at
-- registration and on every rotation, so the mismatch aborts at config time
-- rather than at the first transfer.
factoryMatchesConfig : NttTokenConfig -> NttFactory -> Bool
factoryMatchesConfig config factory = case (config, factory) of
  (LockUnlock, TransferFactoryCid _) -> True
  (BurnMint, BurnMintFactoryCid _) -> True
  _ -> False

-- | The committed transfer factory; aborts if the deployment holds a burn/mint
-- factory (impossible for a registered manager, which checks the mode match).
requireTransferFactory : NttFactory -> Update (ContractId TIV1.TransferFactory)
requireTransferFactory = \case
  TransferFactoryCid cid -> pure cid
  BurnMintFactoryCid _ -> abort "ntt: deployment's committed factory is not a transfer factory"

-- | The committed burn/mint factory; aborts if the deployment holds a transfer
-- factory (impossible for a registered manager, which checks the mode match).
requireBurnMintFactory : NttFactory -> Update (ContractId BurnMintFactory)
requireBurnMintFactory = \case
  BurnMintFactoryCid cid -> pure cid
  TransferFactoryCid _ -> abort "ntt: deployment's committed factory is not a burn/mint factory"

-- | A peer NTT deployment on another chain: its manager address (the
-- @recipientManager@/@sourceManager@ in the transceiver message) and its
-- transceiver address (the emitter of inbound VAAs). Both 32 bytes.
data Peer = Peer with
    managerAddress     : Bytes32
    transceiverAddress : Bytes32
  deriving (Eq, Show)

normalizePeer : Peer -> Peer
normalizePeer p = Peer with
    managerAddress = normalizeHex p.managerAddress
    transceiverAddress = normalizeHex p.transceiverAddress

-- | Domain-separation tag for NTT manager addresses.
nttManagerAddressTag : Text
nttManagerAddressTag = "wormhole:ntt-manager:v1"

-- | The 32-byte NTT manager address of the 'NttManager' allocated at
-- @managerId@ for @(operator, namespace)@. Bound to the namespace, not the
-- admin, so it survives 'TransferAdmin'.
nttManagerAddressFor : Party -> Party -> Int -> Bytes32
nttManagerAddressFor operator namespace managerId =
  derivedAddress (DomainTag nttManagerAddressTag) operator namespace (RegistryId managerId)

-- | Domain-separation tag for NTT recipient-address bindings.
recipientAddressTag : Text
recipientAddressTag = "wormhole:ntt-recipient:v1"

-- | @keccak256(tag ‖ lp(utf8(recipientText)))@. Split from
-- 'recipientAddressFor' so tests can pin the encoding against a fixed string.
recipientAddressFromText : Text -> Bytes32
recipientAddressFromText recipientText =
  normalizeHex (keccak256 (toHex recipientAddressTag
    <> unLenPrefixed (lenPrefixed (toHex recipientText))))

-- | The 32-byte binding a sender computes off-chain for an outbound transfer's
-- @recipientAddress@ to reach @recipient@ on Canton. The inbound path
-- recomputes it from the caller-supplied @recipient@ and checks it against the
-- VAA.
recipientAddressFor : Party -> Bytes32
recipientAddressFor recipient = recipientAddressFromText (partyToText recipient)

-- | Domain-separation tag for burn/mint instrument-id bindings.
nttInstrumentIdTag : Text
nttInstrumentIdTag = "wormhole:ntt-instrument:v1"

-- | Text form of 'nttInstrumentIdFor'. Split out so tests can pin the encoding
-- against a fixed string.
nttInstrumentIdFromText : Text -> Int -> Text
nttInstrumentIdFromText namespaceText nonce =
  "wormhole-ntt:" <> normalizeHex (keccak256 (toHex nttInstrumentIdTag
    <> unLenPrefixed (lenPrefixed (toHex namespaceText))
    <> intToBytesN nonce 8))

-- | The instrument id a burn/mint deployment's instrument must carry:
-- hash-committed to the registering @namespace@ (the deployment's stable
-- identity, not the transferable admin), with @nonce@ distinguishing multiple
-- deployments by the same namespace.
--
-- This binding is what makes a burn/mint instrument exclusive to one
-- deployment. Registration is permissionless and gg administers every
-- burn/mint instrument, so without it anyone could register a second manager
-- for an instrument that is already bridged and mint it against VAAs from a
-- peer they control. A CIP-56 instrument's id is part of its identity and
-- fixed at creation, so committing the namespace into the id at creation and
-- checking the commitment at registration ('RegisterManager') means only that
-- namespace can ever bind a manager to the instrument. It plays the role of
-- EVM NTT's @setMinter@ pointer, attached to the immutable instrument identity
-- instead of a mutable token field.
nttInstrumentIdFor : Party -> Int -> Text
nttInstrumentIdFor namespace nonce = nttInstrumentIdFromText (partyToText namespace) nonce

-- | Check that a caller-supplied 'LockedLedger' is this deployment's own, and
-- return its payload. Only 'Wormhole.Ntt.Governance.RegisterManager' can
-- create a ledger (it needs the root's signatures), and it creates exactly one
-- per lock/unlock deployment, so matching the identity fields suffices. The
-- ledger is named by the stable @namespace@, not the transferable @admin@, so
-- it survives 'TransferAdmin' untouched.
resolveLedger : NttManager -> ContractId LockedLedger -> Update LockedLedger
resolveLedger mgr ledgerCid = do
  ledger <- fetch ledgerCid
  assertMsg "ntt: locked ledger does not belong to this deployment"
    (ledger.operator == mgr.operator
      && ledger.namespace == mgr.namespace
      && ledger.guardianGovernance == mgr.guardianGovernance
      && ledger.managerAddress == mgr.managerAddress)
  pure ledger

-- | Validate custody holdings (owned by the deployment's current @admin@ —
-- the custodian — this deployment's instrument, unlocked) and return their
-- total. Run over every custody holding the manager spends: the choice bodies
-- carry all four signatures, so without this check a caller could steer a
-- choice into spending another signatory's holdings on ambient authority
-- alone.
sumCustodyHoldings : NttManager -> [ContractId Holding] -> Update Decimal
sumCustodyHoldings mgr cids = do
  amounts <- forA cids \cid -> do
    hv <- view <$> fetch cid
    assertMsg "ntt: custody holding is not owned by the custodian"
      (hv.owner == mgr.admin)
    assertMsg "ntt: custody holding instrument mismatch"
      (hv.instrumentId == mgr.instrumentId)
    assertMsg "ntt: custody holding is locked" (hv.lock == None)
    pure hv.amount
  pure (sum amounts)

-- | The hint stored back on the ledger after an operation: the single
-- resulting holding, or 'None' when the registry returned zero or several
-- (then 'ConsolidateCustody' can rebuild it).
custodyHintFrom : [ContractId Holding] -> Optional (ContractId Holding)
custodyHintFrom = \case
  [cid] -> Some cid
  _ -> None

-- | The inbound VAA verification shared by 'Release' and 'Mint' — the
-- security-critical gate, run entirely against the manager's own committed
-- configuration. In order:
--
--   1. Pin the disclosed 'CoreState' to the deployment's committed guardian
--      trust anchor (its @guardianGovernance@). Pinning the operator instead
--      would not help: a compromised operator can co-sign a forged 'CoreState'
--      under a throwaway gg party, but cannot forge the real gg's signature.
--   2. Verify the VAA's guardian signatures and consume its digest in the
--      deployment's replay trie (scoped to the stable @namespace@, so replay
--      protection is unaffected by 'TransferAdmin'), atomically.
--   3. Check the VAA emitter is the configured peer transceiver for its chain,
--      and the nested message's source and recipient managers match the peer
--      and this deployment.
--   4. Bind the caller-supplied @recipient@ to the VAA's @recipientAddress@,
--      so a relayer cannot steer the payout.
--
-- Returns the decoded transfer.
verifyInboundTransfer
  :  NttManager
  -> Party                    -- ^ executor (relayer; carries no authority)
  -> ContractId CoreState
  -> ContractId ReplayNode
  -> Bytes                    -- ^ the encoded VAA
  -> [(Int, Bytes)]           -- ^ guardian pubkey hints (untrusted)
  -> Party                    -- ^ recipient, bound against the VAA
  -> Update NativeTokenTransfer
verifyInboundTransfer mgr executor coreStateCid replayNodeCid vaaBytes pubKeys recipient = do
  csGg <- exercise coreStateCid GetGuardianGovernance with reader = executor
  assertMsg "ntt: core state is not the committed guardian trust anchor"
    (csGg == mgr.guardianGovernance)
  (vaa, _node) <- exercise coreStateCid VerifyAndConsumeVAA with
    encodedVAA = vaaBytes
    pubKeys
    consumer = mgr.namespace
    replayNodeCid
  let peer = fromSomeNote ("ntt: no peer for source chain " <> show vaa.emitterChain)
               (Map.lookup vaa.emitterChain mgr.peers)
  assertMsg "ntt: VAA emitter is not the configured peer transceiver"
    (normalizeHex vaa.emitterAddress == peer.transceiverAddress)
  let wm = decodeWormholeTransceiverMessage vaa.payload
  assertMsg "ntt: wrong recipient manager" (wm.recipientManager == mgr.managerAddress)
  assertMsg "ntt: source manager is not the peer manager"
    (wm.sourceManager == peer.managerAddress)
  let mm = decodeNttManagerMessage wm.managerPayload
      ntt = decodeNativeTokenTransfer mm.payload
  assertMsg "ntt: recipient does not match VAA recipientAddress"
    (recipientAddressFor recipient == normalizeHex ntt.recipientAddress)
  pure ntt

template NttManager
  with
    operator             : Party
    namespace            : Party           -- stable identity: address preimages, replay scope, emitter owner
    admin                : Party           -- operational role and lock/unlock custodian; transferable
    guardianGovernance   : Party           -- gg: mint authority and trust anchor
    managerId            : Int             -- allocated by NttGovernance; in the address preimage
    managerAddress       : Bytes32         -- nttManagerAddressFor operator namespace managerId
    transceiverEmitterId : Int             -- the deployment's transceiver Emitter (owner = namespace)
    instrumentId         : InstrumentId    -- the bridged CIP-56 instrument
    tokenConfig          : NttTokenConfig
    tokenDecimals        : Int             -- the local token's decimals (0..10)
    peers                : Map Int Peer    -- chain id -> peer deployment
    factory              : NttFactory      -- the registry's committed factory, typed by mode
  where
    -- See the module header for what each signature contributes. All four are
    -- inherited from the NttGovernance root at registration (namespace and
    -- admin from the registering caller); no signatory acts per transfer.
    signatory operator, namespace, admin, guardianGovernance

    -- | Register or replace a peer deployment. Consuming, but only admin
    -- config changes it, so the manager cid is stable across transfer traffic.
    choice SetPeer : ContractId NttManager
      with
        chainId : Int
        peer    : Peer
      controller admin
      do create this with peers = Map.insert chainId (normalizePeer peer) peers

    -- | Repoint the committed registry factory. Needed only when the registry
    -- is upgraded and re-creates its factory under a new cid; @admin@ resolves
    -- the new cid from the registry and commits it here. On a gg-adminned
    -- deployment this is automatically a governance action.
    choice SetFactory : ContractId NttManager
      with
        newFactory : NttFactory
      controller admin
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: factory type does not match the token config"
          (factoryMatchesConfig tokenConfig newFactory)
        create this with factory = newFactory

    -- | Hand the operational role — and, for lock/unlock, the reserve it owns —
    -- to @newAdmin@. Both admins authorize: the old admin resigns, and the new
    -- admin accepts what it is inheriting, naming the committed factory it
    -- vetted (@vettedFactory@ must match). In practice the handoff runs as
    -- propose-accept ('AdminTransferProposal' carries the old admin's standing
    -- consent and its 'AcceptAdminTransfer' nests this choice), so the parties
    -- need not act in one submission; exercising this directly with both
    -- authorities works too. The pot is transferred to @newAdmin@ and the
    -- ledger's hint repointed. The pot must cover the ledger balance:
    -- custody the hint does not track — untracked fragments, or a reserve
    -- eroded by registry fees — would stay with the old admin, out of the
    -- deployment's reach once the role changes hands. If the coverage check
    -- fails, run 'ConsolidateCustody' first; the outgoing admin can include
    -- extra holdings of its own there to cover a shortfall. The deployment's
    -- identity — manager and transceiver addresses,
    -- replay scope, the ledger — stays with the unchanged @namespace@, so
    -- nothing changes on the wire, no replay window opens, and the ledger
    -- contract is untouched.
    --
    -- Transferring to @guardianGovernance@ is the quorum's custody opt-in: the
    -- reserve becomes quorum-owned and every admin operation ('SetPeer',
    -- 'SetFactory', a further transfer) becomes a governance action. The same
    -- choice serves admin succession between ordinary parties, and lets gg
    -- hand the role back (demotion).
    choice TransferAdmin : (ContractId NttManager, Optional (ContractId LockedLedger))
      with
        newAdmin      : Party
        ledgerCid     : Optional (ContractId LockedLedger)  -- required for lock/unlock
        vettedFactory : NttFactory
        extraArgs     : ExtraArgs
      controller admin, newAdmin
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: admin transfer to the current admin" (newAdmin /= admin)
        assertMsg "ntt: committed factory is not the one the new admin vetted"
          (factory == vettedFactory)
        ledgerCid' <- case tokenConfig of
          BurnMint -> pure None
          LockUnlock -> do
            let lcid = fromSomeNote
                  "ntt: ledger cid required for a lock/unlock admin transfer" ledgerCid
            ledger <- resolveLedger this lcid
            case ledger.custodyHoldingCid of
              None -> do
                assertMsg "ntt: reserve not consolidated; run ConsolidateCustody before the admin transfer"
                  (ledger.balance == 0.0)
                pure (Some lcid)
              Some pot -> do
                total <- sumCustodyHoldings this [pot]
                assertMsg "ntt: custody pot does not cover the locked balance; run ConsolidateCustody first"
                  (total >= ledger.balance)
                transferFactoryCid <- requireTransferFactory factory
                (movedCids, _) <- executeSyncTransfer transferFactoryCid
                  SyncTransfer with
                    sender = admin
                    receiver = newAdmin
                    amount = total
                    instrumentId
                  [pot]
                  extraArgs
                Some <$> exercise lcid SetCustodyHint with
                  newCustodyHint = custodyHintFrom movedCids
        mgrCid' <- create this with admin = newAdmin
        pure (mgrCid', ledgerCid')

    -- | Outbound transfer. Trims the amount to the wire form, moves @user@'s
    -- tokens (lock into the custodian's reserve, crediting the deployment's
    -- 'LockedLedger', or burn, returning change), and publishes the NTT message
    -- through the deployment's transceiver 'Emitter'.
    --
    -- @user@ is the sole controller and self-pays the governance message fee
    -- (as payer it co-controls the nested 'PublishMessage', which draws the
    -- fee from @feeAllocation@). Everything else the body exercises runs on
    -- the manager's inherited signatory authority; the CIP-56 factory checks
    -- that the input holdings belong to @user@.
    --
    -- The message id is the transceiver's next sequence number, read from the
    -- fetched 'Emitter' in the same transaction that publishes, so it equals
    -- the resulting VAA's sequence and is unique per transceiver.
    nonconsuming choice Transfer : WormholeMessage
      with
        user                  : Party
        transceiverEmitterCid : ContractId Emitter
        coreStateCid          : ContractId CoreState
        ledgerCid             : Optional (ContractId LockedLedger)  -- required for lock/unlock
        recipientChain        : Int
        recipientAddress      : Bytes32
        sourceToken           : Bytes32
        rawAmount             : Int      -- integer units at tokenDecimals
        nonce                 : Int
        consistencyLevel      : Int
        inputHoldingCids      : [ContractId Holding]
        feeAllocation         : Optional (ContractId Allocation, ExtraArgs)  -- message fee; None at fee 0
        extraArgs             : ExtraArgs
      controller user
      do
        let peer = fromSomeNote ("ntt: no peer for chain " <> show recipientChain)
                     (Map.lookup recipientChain peers)
            trimmed = trimAmount rawAmount tokenDecimals
            amount = trimmedToDecimal trimmed
        assertMsg "ntt: transfer amount is zero after trimming" (amount > 0.0)
        transceiver <- fetch transceiverEmitterCid
        assertMsg "ntt: transceiver emitter is not this deployment's"
          (transceiver.owner == namespace
            && transceiver.operator == operator
            && transceiver.emitterId == transceiverEmitterId)
        case tokenConfig of
          -- Lock: move the user's holdings into the custodian's reserve, merge
          -- the fresh deposit with the existing pot so the deployment keeps a
          -- single custody holding, and credit the ledger — all atomically.
          LockUnlock -> do
            let lcid = fromSomeNote
                  "ntt: ledger cid required for a lock/unlock transfer" ledgerCid
                custodian = admin
            transferFactoryCid <- requireTransferFactory factory
            ledger <- resolveLedger this lcid
            (depositCids, _) <- executeSyncTransfer transferFactoryCid
              SyncTransfer with
                sender = user
                receiver = custodian
                amount
                instrumentId
              inputHoldingCids
              extraArgs
            let potCids = case ledger.custodyHoldingCid of
                  Some pot -> pot :: depositCids
                  None -> depositCids
            newHint <- case potCids of
              -- Already a single holding (first lock, or an empty pot).
              [single] -> pure (Some single)
              _ -> do
                total <- sumCustodyHoldings this potCids
                (mergedCids, _) <- executeSyncTransfer transferFactoryCid
                  SyncTransfer with
                    sender = custodian
                    receiver = custodian
                    amount = total
                    instrumentId
                  potCids
                  extraArgs
                pure (custodyHintFrom mergedCids)
            _ <- exercise lcid Credit with amount, newCustodyHint = newHint
            pure ()
          -- Burn: spend the user's holdings for exactly the wire amount and
          -- return the rest as change.
          BurnMint -> do
            burnMintFactoryCid <- requireBurnMintFactory factory
            holdingAmounts <- forA inputHoldingCids \cid -> do
              hv <- view <$> fetch cid
              assertMsg "ntt: burn holding not owned by sender" (hv.owner == user)
              assertMsg "ntt: burn holding instrument mismatch" (hv.instrumentId == instrumentId)
              assertMsg "ntt: burn holding is locked" (hv.lock == None)
              pure hv.amount
            let total = sum holdingAmounts
            assertMsg "ntt: input holdings do not cover the burn amount" (total >= amount)
            let change = total - amount
                outputs =
                  if change == 0.0
                    then []
                    else [ BurnMintOutput with owner = user, amount = change, context = extraArgs.context ]
            _ <- exercise burnMintFactoryCid BurnMintFactory_BurnMint with
              expectedAdmin = instrumentId.admin
              instrumentId
              inputHoldingCids
              outputs
              extraActors = [user]
              extraArgs
            pure ()
        let ntt = NativeTokenTransfer with
              decimals = trimmed.decimals
              amount = trimmed.amount
              sourceToken = normalizeHex sourceToken
              recipientAddress = normalizeHex recipientAddress
              recipientChain
              additionalPayload = ""
            mm = NttManagerMessage with
              id = leftPadTo 32 (intToBytesN transceiver.sequence 8)
              sender = managerAddress
              payload = encodeNativeTokenTransfer ntt
            wm = WormholeTransceiverMessage with
              sourceManager = managerAddress
              recipientManager = peer.managerAddress
              managerPayload = encodeNttManagerMessage mm
              transceiverPayload = ""
        exercise transceiverEmitterCid PublishMessage with
          payload = encodeWormholeTransceiverMessage wm
          nonce
          consistencyLevel
          coreStateCid
          payer = user
          feeAllocation

    -- | Inbound unlock (lock/unlock deployments), permissionless. Any
    -- @executor@ relays the VAA; 'verifyInboundTransfer' is the gate. The
    -- transfer spends the deployment's consolidated pot (the ledger's custody
    -- hint) unless the caller supplies explicit holdings; the sender change
    -- becomes the new pot. The ledger is then debited, and 'Debit' rejects
    -- anything above the balance, so this deployment can never pay out more
    -- than it locked. Returns the successor ledger cid and the decoded
    -- transfer.
    nonconsuming choice Release : (ContractId LockedLedger, NativeTokenTransfer)
      with
        executor         : Party
        coreStateCid     : ContractId CoreState
        replayNodeCid    : ContractId ReplayNode
        vaaBytes         : Bytes
        pubKeys          : [(Int, Bytes)]
        recipient        : Party
        ledgerCid        : ContractId LockedLedger
        inputHoldingCids : [ContractId Holding]      -- explicit override; [] = use the pot hint
        extraArgs        : ExtraArgs
      controller executor
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: release is only for lock/unlock deployments"
          (tokenConfig == LockUnlock)
        ledger <- resolveLedger this ledgerCid
        ntt <- verifyInboundTransfer this executor coreStateCid replayNodeCid
                 vaaBytes pubKeys recipient
        let wireAmount = TrimmedAmount with decimals = ntt.decimals, amount = ntt.amount
            releaseAmount = untrimToDecimal wireAmount tokenDecimals
        assertMsg "ntt: received amount is zero at this token's decimals"
          (releaseAmount > 0.0)
        assertMsg "ntt: release exceeds this deployment's locked balance"
          (releaseAmount <= ledger.balance)
        inputs <- case (inputHoldingCids, ledger.custodyHoldingCid) of
          ([], Some pot) -> pure [pot]
          ([], None) -> abort "ntt: no custody hint on the ledger; supply explicit holdings"
          (cids, _) -> pure cids
        -- The body carries every signatory's authority, so validate that the
        -- holdings being spent are the current admin's own — a caller must not
        -- be able to steer the release into another signatory's holdings.
        _ <- sumCustodyHoldings this inputs
        transferFactoryCid <- requireTransferFactory factory
        (_, changeCids) <- executeSyncTransfer transferFactoryCid
          SyncTransfer with
            sender = admin
            receiver = recipient
            amount = releaseAmount
            instrumentId
          inputs
          extraArgs
        ledgerCid' <- exercise ledgerCid Debit with
          amount = releaseAmount
          newCustodyHint = custodyHintFrom changeCids
        pure (ledgerCid', ntt)

    -- | Merge custody holdings back into a single pot and repoint the
    -- ledger's hint at it: the repair path after a registry-side
    -- reorganization, an operation that left multiple change holdings, or
    -- releases that used explicit inputs. A single supplied holding just
    -- repoints the hint, with no registry transfer; two or more are merged
    -- through the factory. This is also the custodian's top-up path: the
    -- holdings are only checked to be the custodian's own of this
    -- instrument, so the admin can pass extra holdings to restore a reserve
    -- eroded by registry fees.
    --
    -- Permissionless, so relayer tooling can run it opportunistically. On a
    -- registry that charges the sender fees the merge itself costs the
    -- reserve money, but a stranger cannot grind it down for free: the
    -- single-holding call moves nothing, and a merge needs a second
    -- admin-owned holding, which the stranger must donate. The holdings
    -- carry no deployment tag, so callers should pass this deployment's own
    -- fragments; merging another deployment's pot is value-neutral but
    -- stales that deployment's hint, which it can repair the same way.
    nonconsuming choice ConsolidateCustody : ContractId LockedLedger
      with
        caller             : Party
        ledgerCid          : ContractId LockedLedger
        custodyHoldingCids : [ContractId Holding]
        extraArgs          : ExtraArgs
      controller caller
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: consolidation is only for lock/unlock deployments"
          (tokenConfig == LockUnlock)
        _ <- resolveLedger this ledgerCid
        case custodyHoldingCids of
          [] -> abort "ntt: no custody holdings supplied"
          [single] -> do
            _ <- sumCustodyHoldings this [single]
            exercise ledgerCid SetCustodyHint with newCustodyHint = Some single
          _ -> do
            total <- sumCustodyHoldings this custodyHoldingCids
            let custodian = admin
            transferFactoryCid <- requireTransferFactory factory
            (mergedCids, _) <- executeSyncTransfer transferFactoryCid
              SyncTransfer with
                sender = custodian
                receiver = custodian
                amount = total
                instrumentId
              custodyHoldingCids
              extraArgs
            exercise ledgerCid SetCustodyHint with
              newCustodyHint = custodyHintFrom mergedCids

    -- | Inbound mint (burn/mint deployments), permissionless. Any @executor@
    -- relays the VAA; 'verifyInboundTransfer' is the gate. The mint runs
    -- inside the recipient's standing 'DepositPreapproval' (checked to match
    -- the recipient and this deployment's instrument), which supplies the
    -- recipient's signature while the manager supplies gg's instrument-admin
    -- authority — so the recipient can be offline. If the recipient has not
    -- opted in, the transaction aborts before the VAA digest is consumed, and
    -- the same VAA stays deliverable later.
    nonconsuming choice Mint : NativeTokenTransfer
      with
        executor              : Party
        coreStateCid          : ContractId CoreState
        replayNodeCid         : ContractId ReplayNode
        vaaBytes              : Bytes
        pubKeys               : [(Int, Bytes)]
        recipient             : Party
        depositPreapprovalCid : ContractId DepositPreapproval
        extraArgs             : ExtraArgs
      controller executor
      do
        assertMsg "ntt: mint is only for burn/mint deployments"
          (tokenConfig == BurnMint)
        ntt <- verifyInboundTransfer this executor coreStateCid replayNodeCid
                 vaaBytes pubKeys recipient
        pre <- fetch depositPreapprovalCid
        assertMsg "ntt: deposit pre-approval is not for this recipient"
          (pre.owner == recipient)
        assertMsg "ntt: deposit pre-approval is for a different instrument"
          (pre.instrumentId == instrumentId)
        let wireAmount = TrimmedAmount with decimals = ntt.decimals, amount = ntt.amount
            mintAmount = untrimToDecimal wireAmount tokenDecimals
        assertMsg "ntt: received amount is zero at this token's decimals"
          (mintAmount > 0.0)
        burnMintFactoryCid <- requireBurnMintFactory factory
        exercise depositPreapprovalCid Deposit with
          amount = mintAmount
          factoryCid = burnMintFactoryCid
          extraArgs
        pure ntt

-- | The current admin's standing offer to hand the operational role to
-- @newAdmin@: the propose half of the admin handoff. A single self-signed
-- create by the proposing admin; @newAdmin@ accepts at its own pace with
-- 'AcceptAdminTransfer', or the proposer takes the offer back with
-- 'RetractAdminTransfer'. The offer pins the deployment (@managerAddress@) and
-- the proposing admin, so a proposal goes stale — and rejects — if the role
-- has changed hands since it was made.
template AdminTransferProposal
  with
    admin          : Party    -- the proposing (current) admin
    newAdmin       : Party
    managerAddress : Bytes32  -- binds the offer to one deployment
  where
    signatory admin
    observer newAdmin

    -- | Accept the offer: exercises 'TransferAdmin' on the deployment's
    -- manager, with the old admin's authority supplied by this contract's
    -- stored signature and the new admin's by the controller. The acceptor
    -- names the committed factory it vetted, exactly as in the direct
    -- ceremony.
    choice AcceptAdminTransfer : (ContractId NttManager, Optional (ContractId LockedLedger))
      with
        mgrCid        : ContractId NttManager
        ledgerCid     : Optional (ContractId LockedLedger)  -- required for lock/unlock
        vettedFactory : NttFactory
        extraArgs     : ExtraArgs
      controller newAdmin
      do
        mgr <- fetch mgrCid
        assertMsg "ntt: proposal is not for this deployment"
          (mgr.managerAddress == managerAddress)
        assertMsg "ntt: proposal is not by the deployment's current admin"
          (mgr.admin == admin)
        exercise mgrCid TransferAdmin with newAdmin, ledgerCid, vettedFactory, extraArgs

    -- | The proposer takes the offer back; nothing else changes.
    choice RetractAdminTransfer : ()
      controller admin
      do pure ()
